# mail-muncher — sample crontab entry.
#
# Install with `crontab -e` and paste the line below, adjusting the three
# absolute paths. Cron's environment is not your shell's: $PATH is minimal and
# $HOME may be unset, so spell out the binary, the config and the log file in
# full rather than relying on `mail-muncher` or `~` resolving.
#
# One cycle every ten minutes:

*/10 * * * * /usr/local/bin/mail-muncher run --config /Users/YOUR-USERNAME/.config/mail-muncher/config.yml >> /Users/YOUR-USERNAME/.local/state/mail-muncher/cron.log 2>&1

# Overlapping invocations are safe, and that is by design.
#
# Both `run` and `daemon` take the same lockfile — <state_dir>/mail-muncher.lock
# — for the whole of each cycle. If a cycle is still going when the next one
# fires (a big first sync, a slow network), the newcomer does not wait, does not
# fetch, and above all does not write: it exits 3 immediately with "lock held by
# another process". Nothing is fetched twice and no state file is corrupted by a
# half-written cursor. Exit 3 in the log means "the previous one was still
# going", not "something broke" — there is nothing to fix, and the next tick
# will pick things up.
#
# The same lock is why you can run this crontab entry alongside
# `mail-muncher daemon`, or on two machines sharing a state directory over a
# network mount, without the two racing each other.
#
# Other exit statuses worth alerting on:
#   1  config or validation error — the file will not load or a rule will not
#      compile. It will fail identically every ten minutes until you fix it.
#   2  provider or auth failure, typically an expired OAuth token. Run
#      `mail-muncher auth` once, interactively, to refresh it.
#
# Prefer a long-running process to a cron entry? On macOS use the launchd agent
# in ./launchd/ instead — it restarts on failure and survives logout.

# Machine-readable output: add --json and send stdout somewhere an agent can
# tail. Manifests go to stdout, logs to stderr, so the two never interleave:
#
# */10 * * * * /usr/local/bin/mail-muncher run --json --config /Users/YOUR-USERNAME/.config/mail-muncher/config.yml >> /Users/YOUR-USERNAME/.local/state/mail-muncher/manifests.ndjson 2>> /Users/YOUR-USERNAME/.local/state/mail-muncher/cron.log
